/*! \page developer_backend_page CUDA vs. C++ Backends

\ref developer_container_page (Prev)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_test_page      (Next)
<BR>

The PSCF package builds two executable files, that are pscf_rpc and 
pscf_rpg, that are designed to solve the same set of problems involving 
real periodic fields, but that are specialized to use different hardware. 
The pscf_rpg executable performs makes use of a GPU, while the pscf_rpg 
executable uses only a CPU. 

The key difference in the implementation of these two programs arises 
from differences in:

   - Where are arrays of data stored (CPU vs. GPU memory) ?

   - Where are mathematical operations on large arrays performed?

In pscf_rpc, all data structures that use arrays that are allocated on
the heap memory use arrays that are allocated in CPU main memory, and
all computations are performed on the CPU.
In pscf_rpg, most large array data structures are instead allocated
in GPU global memory, and all basic mathematical operations that are 
performed on these arrays are performed directly on the GPU. 
In both cases, mathematical operations that are performed on arrays
include elementwise mathematical operations on arrays such a 
vecctor-vector addition and scalar-vector multiplication, array 
reductions, and fast Fourier transforms.  

We describe these differences in what follows by saying that analogous 
executable files use different computational "backends". The system
used by PSCF to support the use of more than one backend relies on 
heavy use of C++ class templates.  The subpages linked below describe 
this system.

